.TH CBMAP_BEGIN_ITER 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cbmap_begin_iter \- create an iterator positioned at an ordered map's first entry
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cmap_iterator *cbmap_begin_iter(cbmap cbm, char **err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Allocates an iterator at the first element of the map. Each step gives
the key and the value of one element. The caller reads them through the
unified accessors ccol_iter_key_ptr(3) and ccol_iter_val_ptr(3). The
value pointer points into the storage of the map itself. A write through
that pointer therefore updates the stored value in place.

The generic ccol_begin(3) macro selects this function for a cbmap. Call
this function directly only when you already know the container type and
you want the error string. ccol_begin(3) is the ordinary entry point.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cbm   Ordered map to iterate over, or NULL
err   Optional pointer that receives an error string on failure, or
      NULL
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a pointer to a new iterator at the first element.
Returns NULL when there is nothing to iterate over, and sets *err to
NULL. This happens when cbm is NULL or when the map holds no elements.
Returns NULL and sets *err to an error string when the library cannot
allocate the iterator.
.fi
.SH "ITERATION ORDER"
.nf
The walk goes in order over the AVL tree. It therefore visits the
elements in ascending key order. It uses the same order that the map
itself uses to place a key. A hash map gives no such order. This order is
part of the contract of the container, and the caller can depend on it.
It is the reason to choose cbmap and not chmap when the order matters.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The function sets *err to NULL on entry, before anything else. To
separate an empty result from a failure, test *err and not the return
value
A NULL cbm iterates as an empty map. The function reports no error. This
is a deliberate contract across modules. chashmap_begin_iter(3) and
cvector_begin_iter(3) share it. A map field can still be NULL because the
caller put nothing into it yet. The caller can iterate such a field
directly, and no caller needs its own NULL guard first
The iterator frees itself when ccol_iter_next(3) steps past the last
element and returns NULL. A walk that stops early must end with a call to
ccol_iter_destroy(3). The caller can also declare the iterator with
ccol_iter_declare(3), which destroys it at the exit of the scope
The iterator carries a fixed-size stack for the walk. That stack has room
for the maximum height that an AVL tree of any element count can reach. A
new iterator therefore costs one allocation, and each step costs none
The library allocates the iterator through the memory management
procedures of the map
An insert into the map, or a removal from it, during the walk makes the
iterator invalid. A change of a value in place through
ccol_iter_val_ptr(3) does not
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cbmap_construct(ages, char *, int);
int age = 30;
cbmap_insert(ages, "alice", age);
age = 41;
cbmap_insert(ages, "bob", age);

char *err = NULL;
ccol_iter_declare(ages, it);
for (it = cbmap_begin_iter(ages, &err); it; it = ccol_iter_next(it)) {
    printf("%s = %d\en", *ccol_iter_key_ptr(it),
                         *ccol_iter_val_ptr(it));
}
if (err) {
    fprintf(stderr, "cannot iterate: %s\en", err);
}
cbmap_destroy(ages);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_begin (3),
.BR ccol_iter_next (3),
.BR ccol_iter_destroy (3),
.BR ccol_iter_declare (3),
.BR chashmap_begin_iter (3),
.BR cvector_begin_iter (3)
